2.2 Coordinator Architecture — Why Two Sticks?

The ZBT‑2 runs exactly one protocol at a time — Zigbee OR Thread.
Nabu Casa’s official guidance states that multiprotocol operation “doesn’t work well, and we don’t plan to implement it”. Community testing confirms that trying to run both on the same stick leads to firmware flash failures, device dropouts, and network instability.

Your baseline system uses one ZBT‑2 dedicated to Zigbee, locked to firmware 7.4.4.6 — the most stable build for 2026.
If you later add Matter‑over‑Thread devices, plug in a second coordinator (a second ZBT‑2 re‑flashed with OpenThread RCP, or a Sonoff ZBDongle‑E, or use an existing Apple HomePod / Google Nest Hub as a Thread Border Router). The architecture supports graceful expansion without ever touching your stable Zigbee network.

2.3 Protocol Stack

Layer Technology
Application Home Assistant automations, MQTT, REST API
Integration Zigbee2MQTT v2.0.0 (serial‑to‑MQTT bridge)
Transport MQTT over local TCP
Network Zigbee PRO (IEEE 802.15.4) mesh + Wi‑Fi 802.11 b/g/n/ac
PHY / Data Link O‑QPSK at 2.4 GHz

All data stays inside your home network. No packets ever leave unless you explicitly configure an optional remote access service (like the free Tailscale VPN).

2.4 Data Flow — Example: Temperature Alert

1. Aqara temperature sensor measures 27.2 °C and transmits to the nearest Zigbee router (a light bulb).
2. Router forwards the packet through the mesh to the ZBT‑2 Zigbee coordinator.
3. Zigbee2MQTT parses the frame and publishes an MQTT message:
      zigbee2mqtt/living_room_temp { "temperature": 27.2 }
4. Home Assistant’s MQTT integration updates the entity sensor.living_room_temperature.
5. An automation triggered on numeric_state above: 26 sends a notification to your phone.

End‑to‑end latency: under 1 second. No internet required.

