5. Step‑by‑Step Installation

5.1 Hub Setup — Home Assistant Green

1. Unpack the Green. Connect the included 12 V power adapter and Ethernet cable.
2. Label your ZBT‑2 sticks if you plan to use two (one for Zigbee, one for future Thread).
   · Use a label maker on the USB base and cable.
   · Port convention: Zigbee → top USB‑A. Thread → bottom (leave empty for now).
   · After boot, verify: Settings → System → Hardware → All Hardware → note the /dev/serial/by-id/ path for each stick.
3. Wait 2 minutes for first boot. Open http://homeassistant.local:8123.
4. Follow the onboarding wizard: name your home, create an account, set location and timezone.
5. The ZBT‑2 is auto‑detected. Choose Zigbee2MQTT as your integration.

✅ Hub is live.

5.2 Zigbee Channel Assignment

Wi‑Fi and Zigbee share the 2.4 GHz band. Choose a Zigbee channel that does not overlap with your Wi‑Fi:

Your Wi‑Fi Channel Safe Zigbee Channels
1 (2412 MHz) 11–14
6 (2437 MHz) 15–20
11 (2462 MHz) 21–24

Set the channel in Zigbee2MQTT → Settings → channel:.

5.3 Pairing Order — The Golden Rule

Pair all mains‑powered routers (bulbs) first, in their final locations. Then pair battery‑powered sensors.
This ensures the mesh is fully established before sensors attempt to join.

ThirdReality ZL4 pairing:

1. Screw in the bulb, turn on the wall switch.
2. In Zigbee2MQTT, click “Permit join (All)”.
3. Power‑cycle the bulb rapidly 5 times — it will blink to confirm.
4. The bulb appears within 60 seconds. Rename it immediately.

Aqara sensor pairing:

1. Insert battery, hold the reset button 5 seconds until the LED blinks.
2. With Permit Join active, the sensor appears automatically.
3. Rename and assign it to a room.

5.4 Entity‑Based Automation Rule (Critical)

Always create automations using entity_id, never device_id.

· Wrong: trigger on device “Front Door – Contact Sensor”
· Right: trigger on entity binary_sensor.front_door_contact

Why: If you replace a faulty sensor, device‑based automations break silently. Entity‑based automations survive hardware changes without any editing — you simply assign the same entity_id to the new device.

5.5 Energy Module — Path A (Beginner, Zero Flash)

1. Plug the Sonoff S60ZBTPF into a wall outlet.
2. Press and hold its button for 5 seconds until the LED blinks blue rapidly.
3. In Zigbee2MQTT, click “Permit join”.
4. The plug appears in under 60 seconds. It exposes switch, power, energy, current, and voltage.
5. Immediately check for OTA updates in Zigbee2MQTT → OTA → install v2.0.2 if available.
6. Plug in your appliance and rename the entity.

✅ Energy monitoring is live. No code, no firmware, no risk.

5.6 Zigbee2MQTT v2.0.0 Configuration Migration

If you are upgrading from Zigbee2MQTT v1.x, or if your configuration resets after a restart:

Before upgrading:

· Back up configuration.yaml, database.db, and state.json.
· Add to configuration.yaml:
  ```yaml
  advanced:
    homeassistant_legacy_entity_attributes: false
    homeassistant_legacy_triggers: false
    legacy_api: false
    legacy_availability_payload: false
  device_options:
    legacy: false
  ```

After upgrading:

· Zigbee2MQTT v2.0.0 automatically migrates your config file. The new config starts with version: 4.
· If you see the setup page with no devices, stop the add‑on, restore database.db from backup, set frontend: true in the config, and restart.

