6. Matter & Future‑Proofing

6.1 The State of Matter in 2026

Matter 1.5 (November 2025) enabled cameras, energy management, and standardised tariff data. Thread 1.4 became mandatory for new Border Routers in January 2026. IKEA’s KAJPLATS Matter‑over‑Thread bulbs are now in stores from $5.99.

However, first‑generation Matter devices have proven difficult to commission. The Verge tested six and onboarded only two. Community reports confirm persistent failures.

Prerequisites for adding a Matter‑over‑Thread device (like KAJPLATS):

Requirement Verification Common Failure
IPv6 HA host, phone, and router must have IPv6 enabled. No NAT64. Failed to send IPv6 UDP in OTBR logs
mDNS avahi-daemon must be running. Docker users: --network=host No endpoint available
Phone credential sync iOS/Android Companion App → Troubleshooting → Sync Thread credentials “Thread Border Router needed” despite working OTBR
BLE during commissioning Phone Bluetooth ON; bulb powered on < 15 minutes ago Device not found during QR scan
If credentials mismatch Delete Thread network in HA, re‑create, re‑sync “Credentials do not match”

Our recommendation: Build your core system on Zigbee. It is proven, stable, and local. When Matter matures, your architecture is ready — just plug in a second ZBT‑2 (or use an existing Thread Border Router) and add Matter devices alongside your Zigbee mesh. No rebuilding required.

